@host = http://localhost:5147

### A plain ASP.NET Core route - nothing MCP about it. Just proves the server is up.
GET {{host}}/ping

###
# `server/discover` is the entry point of the 2026-07-28 revision. It replaces
# the `initialize` handshake: instead of negotiating once and keeping a session,
# the client asks "what do you support?" and may cache the answer (`ttlMs`,
# `cacheScope`). Every request from here on is completely self-contained.
#
# Observe in the response: `supportedVersions`, `resultType: "complete"` and the
# `_meta["io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo"]` envelope.
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: server/discover

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "server/discover",
  "params": {
    "_meta": {
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion": "2026-07-28",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo": { "name": "REST Client", "version": "1.0.0" },
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities": {}
    }
  }
}

###
# `tools/list`. No session id anywhere - the `_meta` envelope carries everything
# the server needs to know about this client.
#
# Observe: the result carries the cache hints `ttlMs` and `cacheScope`, which
# every list result may use so that clients can remember the answer.
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: tools/list

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 2,
  "method": "tools/list",
  "params": {
    "_meta": {
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion": "2026-07-28",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo": { "name": "REST Client", "version": "1.0.0" },
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities": {}
    }
  }
}

###
# Call the tool WITHOUT thinking. Nothing happens before the result.
#
# Note the `Mcp-Name` header: `tools/call`, `resources/read` and `prompts/get`
# must name their target in a header so that proxies and gateways can route and
# authorize a request without parsing the JSON body.
#
# Observe: the C# SDK always answers with `Content-Type: text/event-stream` (a
# one-event stream carrying the result) - see the readme, "JSON vs. SSE".
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: tools/call
Mcp-Name: echo-tool

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 3,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "echo-tool",
    "arguments": {
      "message": "Hello World!",
      "thinkHard": false
    },
    "_meta": {
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion": "2026-07-28",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo": { "name": "REST Client", "version": "1.0.0" },
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities": {}
    }
  }
}

###
# The same call WITH `thinkHard` and a `progressToken`. The tool now emits three
# `notifications/progress` messages before its result, so the stream carries
# four SSE events, the result being the last one. THIS is the difference
# Streamable HTTP makes.
#
# The REST Client extension buffers the stream and shows it once it is complete.
# Use `./curl.sh` to watch the events trickle in live, one per second.
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: tools/call
Mcp-Name: echo-tool

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 4,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "echo-tool",
    "arguments": {
      "message": "Hello World!",
      "thinkHard": true
    },
    "_meta": {
      "progressToken": "echo-1",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion": "2026-07-28",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo": { "name": "REST Client", "version": "1.0.0" },
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities": {}
    }
  }
}

###
# Error case 1: a protocol version this server does not implement.
#
# Observe: the dedicated JSON-RPC error `-32022` (UnsupportedProtocolVersion),
# with the list of versions the server actually speaks in
# `error.data.supported`. A client can use that to pick a version it also knows
# instead of just failing. The C# SDK answers this one with HTTP 200 and the
# error inside the SSE stream (the header mismatch below is a real HTTP 400,
# because there the request never reaches the JSON-RPC layer).
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2025-06-18
Mcp-Method: tools/list

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 5,
  "method": "tools/list",
  "params": {
    "_meta": {
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities": {}
    }
  }
}

###
# Error case 2: the `Mcp-Method` header says `tools/list`, the body says
# `tools/call`. Headers and body must agree - otherwise a gateway could be
# tricked into authorizing a harmless list request while a tool call goes
# through.
#
# Observe: HTTP 400 plus JSON-RPC error `-32020` (HeaderMismatch).
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: tools/list
Mcp-Name: echo-tool

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 6,
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "echo-tool",
    "arguments": {
      "message": "Hello World!",
      "thinkHard": false
    },
    "_meta": {
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion": "2026-07-28",
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities": {}
    }
  }
}

###
# The old world, for comparison: a 2025-era client opens with `initialize` and
# no `_meta` envelope at all. The SDK recognizes that and serves it - in
# `Stateless` mode from a fresh server instance per request, so no
# `Mcp-Session-Id` is minted at all. This is why VS Code and other 2025-era
# hosts still work against this server.
#
# Observe: the negotiated `protocolVersion` in the result is a 2025-era version.
# `initialize` never negotiates 2026-07-28 - that era has no handshake.
POST {{host}}/mcp
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json, text/event-stream

{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 1,
  "method": "initialize",
  "params": {
    "protocolVersion": "2025-06-18",
    "capabilities": {},
    "clientInfo": {
      "name": "REST Client",
      "title": "REST Client in VSCode",
      "version": "1.0.0"
    }
  }
}

###
# GET on the MCP endpoint. In the 2025 world this opened the standalone SSE
# stream; in the 2026-07-28 revision that stream is gone and only POST remains.
#
# Observe: HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed. The same goes for DELETE (which used to
# terminate a session).
GET {{host}}/mcp
Accept: text/event-stream
